#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""dacpro-ctl - tune the Raspberry Pi DAC Pro (PCM5242) through the kernel pcm512x driver, plus the PipeWire path feeding it.

  dacpro-ctl show                 current values of every option (saved + live ALSA readback)
  dacpro-ctl verify               JSON: do the DAC's live registers match the saved settings?
  dacpro-ctl list                 every option, allowed values, what it does
  sudo dacpro-ctl set OPT VALUE   apply now + save (survives reboot)
  sudo dacpro-ctl reset           back to defaults
  sudo dacpro-ctl apply           re-apply saved settings (run at boot by dacpro.service)

On the Pi the DAC has a real driver (dtoverlay=rpi-dacpro -> snd_rpi_hifiberry_dacplus / pcm512x), so every
register the old Ventuno build poked over I2C is exposed as an ALSA mixer control. This tool just drives
`amixer`. Settings are stored in /etc/dacpro.json. PipeWire options are written as user config for the
desktop user and need a PipeWire restart (the tool does it for you unless --no-restart is given).
"""
